Tessel 2 / MT7620: reset the shared SPI block only once (k6.6 regression, persists k6.12).

THE 24.10 (kernel 6.6) HARD NON-BOOT ROOT CAUSE -- STILL PRESENT AT 25.12 (kernel 6.12).

The MT7620 has TWO SPI controllers -- spi0: spi@b00 (the s25fl256s boot flash) and
spi1: spi@b40 (the Tessel SAMD21 coprocessor, CS1). In the device tree they share a
SINGLE reset line: both nodes carry resets=<&rstctrl 18> / reset-names="spi".

OpenWrt out-of-tree spi-rt2880 driver (patch 821) was refactored to devm APIs for 6.6
and in the process DROPPED the guard the pre-6.6 driver used around the one-time
hardware reset. At 6.12 the driver is unchanged in this respect: rt2880_spi_probe()
still calls device_reset() UNCONDITIONALLY on every probe (the only 6.12 delta is the
final devm_spi_register_master -> devm_spi_register_controller rename). device_reset()
acquires an *exclusive* reset control and always pulses it, so with two controllers on
one shared reset line the boot sequence is:

  1. spi@b00 (flash) probes -> device_reset() pulses the shared SPI reset -> the flash
     controller is initialised and spi-nor read_id() succeeds (dmesg shows
     "s25fl256s1 (32768 Kbytes)"); the flash is briefly usable.
  2. spi@b40 (coprocessor) probes -> device_reset() pulses the SAME shared reset a
     SECOND time, AFTER the flash controller is already up -- wiping its mode3 / SPI
     arbiter configuration.

From then on every read on the flash controller clocks against a reset/idle block:
MISO idles high, so *all* /dev/mtdN data reads return 0xFF (address-independent).
u-boot reads the flash fine (no double-reset) so the kernel uImage loads; but once
Linux is up the squashfs superblock read returns 0xFF -> no hsqs magic -> squashfs
fill_super never runs (silent, no "SQUASHFS error") -> jffs2 fallback finds no nodes
-> "VFS: Cannot open root device ... error -5" -> panic -> reboot loop. The rt2800
wifi "Invalid RF chipset 0x0000" is the same fault (0xFF EEPROM read).

Hardware-pinned at 24.10/k6.6 via an initramfs RAM-root DIAG (md5 of every /dev/mtdN
read equalled md5(0xFF)); the two-fix respin then booted clean (squashfs mounts, hsqs
magic, wifi RF7620) -- confirming this guard is the fix. The 6.12 driver is
byte-identical in the probe reset path, so the same guard applies.

FIX: restore the pre-6.6 behaviour -- reset the shared SPI block exactly once, on the
first controller to probe. Minimal delta; mirrors the driver hardware-validated at
15.05..23.05 and the 24.10 respin.
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-rt2880.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-rt2880.c
@@ -403,6 +403,8 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static atomic_t hw_reset_count = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+
 static int rt2880_spi_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -442,9 +444,19 @@
 	rs->base = base;
 	rs->clk = clk;
 
-	ret = device_reset(&pdev->dev);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * MT7620 has two SPI controllers (spi@b00 flash, spi@b40 coprocessor)
+	 * sharing one reset line (rstctrl bit 18). device_reset() pulses an
+	 * exclusive reset unconditionally, so without this guard the second
+	 * probe re-resets the shared block after the flash controller is up,
+	 * wiping its config -> every flash read returns 0xFF. Reset the shared
+	 * block exactly once, on the first probe, like the pre-6.6 driver.
+	 */
+	if (atomic_inc_return(&hw_reset_count) == 1) {
+		ret = device_reset(&pdev->dev);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
 
 	return devm_spi_register_controller(dev, master);
 }
